//! lotus/stereo_soft_drive.rye -- ALES133: the soft-clip overdrive carried into stereo, ONE SHARED SHOULDER, the rounded corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class. ALES79 named `soft_drive` -- ALES78's hard
//! clip with its corner rounded: below a KNEE the boosted signal passes clean, between the knee and the ceiling the slope HALVES, and once that shoulder reaches the ceiling it pins -- the warmth of an
//! overdriven tube rather than the buzz of a fuzz, for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME
//! length. This rung carries the soft drive into stereo the plain way, ALES132's sibling: run ALES79's proven mono `soft_drive` on each channel with the SAME gain, knee, and ceiling, so one overdrive
//! lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The nonlinear class holds exactly as ALES132 stated it, honestly: a soft drive is still NOT linear, so a shared shoulder does NOT preserve the inter-channel ratio in general -- each channel bends toward
//! the same ceiling by its OWN level, a louder channel bending harder, the ratio compressing. That is not a bug to design around; it is what a stereo soft drive IS. What the shared map DOES keep, per
//! channel, is the ceiling and sign discipline (an odd waveshaper never flips a sample, never crosses the ceiling); and an identical-channel master still soft-drives to an identical-channel output. A knee
//! AT the ceiling degenerates both channels to ALES132's hard drive -- the two stereo corners are one waveshaper read at two knee positions.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadGain (a zero denominator, a below-unity gain, an oversized numerator), BadKnee (a knee below one sample unit or above the ceiling), BadCeiling (a ceiling outside
//! [1, sample_max]), and BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE either channel is mutated -- every fault depends only on the shared parameters or the shared length, so each check is made ONCE up
//! front, and a refusal never soft-drives one channel and leaves the other dry; otherwise soft-drive both channels over [start, start+count) by num/den through knee into ceil via ALES79's `soft_drive`.
//! SoftDriveError reused whole.
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE STEREO SOFT-DRIVE LAW: each channel equals ALES79's mono `soft_drive` with the same (num, den, knee, ceil) over the same span byte for byte -- a boosted sample below the knee
//! passing clean, one in the shoulder rising at half slope, one past the top pinning to sign(x)-ceil, per channel. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their
//! starting length (a soft drive writes values only). THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW: a soft drive is nonlinear, so a shared shoulder does NOT hold the inter-channel ratio (a 1:2 master driven hard bends
//! both toward the ceiling, the ratio compressing) -- unlike the linear amplitude class; yet the ceiling and sign discipline hold per channel, an identical-channel master stays identical, and a knee AT the
//! ceiling degenerates both channels to ALES132's hard drive byte for byte. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal (BadGain, BadKnee, BadCeiling, BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte
//! untouched and still balanced; a unity drive at the rail is the identity on both, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES133.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-032747_fill-ales133-lotus-stereo-soft-drive.md
//! Stands on: lotus/soft_drive.rye (ALES79) -- the mono soft-clip overdrive run once per channel, itself ALES78's pre-gain and ceiling with a knee opened below it; lotus/stereo_drive.rye (ALES132) -- the
//! hard-drive sibling this rounds; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES79's own soft_drive, never a length,
//! never a byte fabricated; the drive gain is a rational num/den (unity = 1/1), the knee and ceiling magnitudes in sample units NOT decibels, the shape a piecewise-linear waveshaper, instantaneous (no
//! attack/release, no anti-aliasing). No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_soft_drive.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const drive = @import("drive.rye");
const soft_drive_mod = @import("soft_drive.rye");

pub const stereo_soft_drive_version = "20260815.032747";

/// Stereo soft drive's faults are exactly ALES79's own `SoftDriveError` -- `BadGain` (zero denominator, below-unity gain, oversized numerator), `BadKnee` (a knee below one sample unit or above the ceiling),
/// `BadCeiling` (a ceiling outside [1, sample_max]), and `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) -- reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new fault: it only runs the same soft drive on each channel
/// after one shared validation.
pub const StereoSoftDriveError = soft_drive_mod.SoftDriveError;

/// Soft-drive `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc` by the rational gain `num`/`den` (at least unity) through the knee `knee` into the magnitude `ceil`, the SAME shoulder on each, running ALES79's
/// proven mono `soft_drive`. A boosted sample below the knee passes clean, one in the shoulder rises at half slope, one past the top pins to sign(x)-ceil (the rounded corner). Validates both channels once
/// against the shared parameters and length before either is mutated: refuses `BadGain` / `BadKnee` / `BadCeiling` / `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels
/// leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_soft_drive(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, num: u32, den: u32, knee: i32, ceil: i32) StereoSoftDriveError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the gain, knee, ceiling, and span are legal -- refused by name before any write, so a refusal never soft-drives one channel and leaves the other dry. The gain, knee, and ceiling checks
    // depend only on the shared parameters; the span check on the shared length. These mirror ALES79's own guards exactly, so each mono soft drive below is pre-validated to succeed.
    if (den == 0) return error.BadGain;
    if (num < den) return error.BadGain;
    if (num > drive.max_drive_num) return error.BadGain;
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (knee < 1) return error.BadKnee;
    if (knee > ceil) return error.BadKnee;
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono soft drive is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME shoulder on each.
    try soft_drive_mod.soft_drive(&sc.left, start, count, num, den, knee, ceil);
    try soft_drive_mod.soft_drive(&sc.right, start, count, num, den, knee, ceil);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- a soft drive writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SOFT-DRIVE LAW -- each channel equals ALES79's mono soft_drive with the same params byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- left.len == right.len after, both holding their
//     starting length; THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW -- the ceiling and sign hold per channel, a 1:2 master's ratio COMPRESSES under a hard-enough drive, an identical-channel master staying identical, a
//     knee AT the ceiling degenerating both channels to ALES132's hard drive; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- each refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, a unity drive at the rail the
//     identity, count = 0 the identity ----------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO SOFT-DRIVE LAW -- each channel equals ALES79's mono soft_drive with the same params, proven side by side over one span with different per-channel samples; a ×2 drive through a wide knee
    //    passes the small samples, rounds the middle at half slope, and pins the loud ones to ±ceil, per channel.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1400, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 1800, -5000 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 4, 1, 1, 1000, 1500); // ×1 through knee 1000 into 1500

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1400, -3000 });
        try soft_drive_mod.soft_drive(&ml, 0, 4, 1, 1, 1000, 1500);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 1800, -5000 });
        try soft_drive_mod.soft_drive(&mr, 0, 4, 1, 1, 1000, 1500);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely (knee 1000, ceil 1500, unity gain): left 300 clean, 1400->1000+400/2=1200, 3000 past the top->1500; right 100 clean, 1800->1000+800/2=1400, 5000->1500.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 1200, -1500 })) return error.LeftShoulderWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -50, 1400, -1500 })) return error.RightShoulderWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a soft drive writes values only. Proven at an even and an odd length.
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400 }, &[_]i16{ 400, 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&even, 0, 4, 2, 1, 300, 500);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ 300, 200, 100 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&odd, 0, 3, 3, 2, 150, 250);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (odd.left.len != 3) return error.OddLength;
    }

    // 3a. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW -- the ceiling and sign discipline hold per channel: every output magnitude is at most the ceiling and every sign matches the input, on BOTH channels, under a hard
    //     boost through a wide knee. This is the soft drive's honest promise (contrast the linear amplitude class, which preserved a ratio).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 800, -4000, 20 }, &[_]i16{ -30000, 7, -900 });
        const ceil: i16 = 1200;
        try stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 3, 5, 2, 600, ceil); // ×2.5 through knee 600 into 1200
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        const li = [_]i16{ 800, -4000, 20 };
        const ri = [_]i16{ -30000, 7, -900 };
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 3) : (i += 1) {
            if (l[i] > ceil or l[i] < -ceil) return error.LeftCeilingCrossed;
            if (r[i] > ceil or r[i] < -ceil) return error.RightCeilingCrossed;
            if (!((li[i] >= 0 and l[i] >= 0) or (li[i] <= 0 and l[i] <= 0))) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if (!((ri[i] >= 0 and r[i] >= 0) or (ri[i] <= 0 and r[i] <= 0))) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 3b. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW, the ratio COMPRESSES -- a 1:2 master (right twice left) driven hard enough that both pin does NOT keep its 1:2 ratio, because a soft drive is nonlinear: both channels
    //     are boosted past the shoulder's top and pin to the same ceiling, so the loud right no longer stands twice the left. The honest opposite of ALES130/ALES131's shared-ratio image law.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, 1000, 1000 }, &[_]i16{ 2000, 2000, 2000 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 3, 8, 1, 2500, 3000); // ×8: both boosted well past the shoulder top (2·3000−2500=3500), both pin to 3000
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        if (l[0] != 3000 or r[0] != 3000) return error.NonlinearPinWrong;
        if (@as(i32, r[0]) == 2 * @as(i32, l[0])) return error.RatioUnexpectedlyHeld; // the linear law does NOT hold here — that is the point
    }

    // 3c. an identical-channel master stays identical through the soft drive -- a shared shoulder on equal inputs gives equal outputs (a mono-in-stereo signal never splits, even under a nonlinear map).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 4, 4, 1, 700, 1000);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3d. A KNEE AT THE CEILING IS ALES132's HARD DRIVE -- with knee == ceil the shoulder vanishes and the stereo soft drive reproduces the stereo hard drive byte for byte, on both channels, over a signal
    //     that both passes and pins. The two stereo corners are one waveshaper read at two knee positions (the family recognition, lifted to stereo).
    {
        const sl = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000 };
        const sr = [_]i16{ 100, -50, 5000, -6000 };
        var soft = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&soft, &sl, &sr);
        var hard = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&hard, &sl, &sr);
        try stereo_soft_drive(&soft, 0, 4, 4, 1, 1500, 1500); // knee AT the ceiling
        // ALES132's hard drive run per channel by hand (drive is the mono hard clip stereo_drive itself composes).
        try drive.drive(&hard.left, 0, 4, 4, 1, 1500);
        try drive.drive(&hard.right, 0, 4, 4, 1, 1500);
        if (!eq(soft.left.samples(), hard.left.samples())) return error.KneeAtCeilLeftNotHard;
        if (!eq(soft.right.samples(), hard.right.samples())) return error.KneeAtCeilRightNotHard;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad gain -- a below-unity gain refuses BadGain, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        if (stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 3, 1, 2, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.BadGainSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongBadGain;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 })) return error.BadGainLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.BadGainRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadGainUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad knee -- a knee above the ceiling refuses BadKnee, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 3, 2, 1 });
        if (stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 3, 2, 1, 1001, 1000)) |_| return error.BadKneeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadKnee) return error.WrongBadKnee;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.BadKneeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 3, 2, 1 })) return error.BadKneeRightMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad ceiling -- a zero ceiling refuses BadCeiling, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 5, 4 });
        if (stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 0, 3, 2, 1, 1, 0)) |_| return error.BadCeilingSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongBadCeiling;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.BadCeilingLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 5, 4 })) return error.BadCeilingRightMutated;
    }

    // 4d. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span -- a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 });
        if (stereo_soft_drive(&sc, 1, 3, 2, 1, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 6, 5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
    }

    // 4e. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- a unity drive at the rail through a rail-high knee is the identity on both channels (nothing boosted past the ceiling, the shoulder never engaged), and count = 0 is the
    //     identity on both.
    {
        var rail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&rail, &[_]i16{ 500, -900, 32767 }, &[_]i16{ -32767, 250, -500 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&rail, 0, 3, 1, 1, timeline.sample_max, timeline.sample_max); // unity, knee and ceiling at the rail
        if (!eq(rail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 500, -900, 32767 })) return error.UnityLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(rail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -32767, 250, -500 })) return error.UnityRightChanged;

        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_soft_drive(&noop, 1, 0, 4, 1, 500, 1000); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-soft-drive: the SOFT-CLIP OVERDRIVE CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED SHOULDER, the rounded corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class — soft-drive a span in both channels of a " ++
        "master by the same gain through the same knee into the same ceiling, running ALES79's proven mono soft_drive on each, so one overdrive lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is a " ++
        "StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. The nonlinear class holds as ALES132 stated it, honestly: a soft drive is not linear, so the same " ++
        "shoulder does NOT hold the inter-channel ratio — each channel bends toward the same ceiling by its own level, a louder channel bending harder, the ratio compressing; that is what a stereo soft " ++
        "drive IS. What the shared map keeps, per channel, is the ceiling and sign discipline, an identical-channel master stays identical, and a knee AT the ceiling degenerates both channels to " ++
        "ALES132's hard drive byte for byte. It validates both channels once against the shared parameters and length before either is mutated (BadGain, BadKnee, BadCeiling, BadRange), so a refusal " ++
        "never soft-drives one channel and leaves the other dry; SoftDriveError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO SOFT-DRIVE LAW: each channel equals ALES79's mono soft_drive with the " ++
        "same params byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. THE PER-CHANNEL NONLINEAR LAW: the ceiling and sign hold per channel, a " ++
        "1:2 master's ratio compresses under a hard drive, an identical-channel master staying identical, a knee at the ceiling reproducing the hard drive. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal " ++
        "leaves both channels untouched and balanced, a unity drive at the rail the identity, count = 0 the identity. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips soft-driven through the proven mono soft " ++
        "drive, the gain a rational num/den and the knee and ceiling sample magnitudes not decibels; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
